{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1533355-v20-true-faith-and-violence","id":1533355,"title":"[V20] True Faith and Violence","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-03-29T03:45:20-07:00","id":1533355,"title":"[V20] True Faith and Violence","content":"So, True Faith is always linked to an high humanity. I'm talking about the typical faith of a pius person. It's so deep this root that in the core manual the requirements is to have humanity 9 and you'll lose the benefits of the faith if this level drop. Of course this is only a point of view and I think there can be other options (mad monks? blind lawful inquisitors?), but mainly in this medium people with True Faith are depicted so pius that they feel bad for thinking about something evil (humanity 9-10) o zelous fighter of the dark forces (from the middle age Inquisition).<br /> <br /> How do you deal with this? Making a practical example from the canon: in Cairo there is a religious Nosferatu (Petra) with true faith that want to protect a girl (Afifa, the Herald) and in doing so, while escaping a group of Setite, she in frenzy slaughter her assailants in an alley. Slaughter. Humanity 9. So doing something bad against someone evil is still in line I think. (Ok, she was in frenzy but nothing caused it, maybe the stress, so is it not clear if it was her choice or not).<br /> <br /> This is of course only one example. If you think of the extreme violence of the inquisition you will find something similar.<br /> <br /> In conclusion, moral questions are always good in Vampire but since they tied a power (True Faith) to morale I see some problems in applying it. My conclusion is that there is no need to tied True Faith and Humanity. Being invested by divine power OR being a very good person that dislike doing bad things are different characteristics. A good person will have to choose to do something harmful at a certain point, otherwise is not doing anything about the evil in the world. Evil agains evil creatures? Mabybe ok. Evil against they controlled servants, in order to survive? Very bad for high levels of humanity.<br /> <br /> Note 1: the last post speaking about this topic is 5 years old, so maybe something changed in your experience and there is something to add to this discourse.<br /> Note 2: for the mods - I tried to post this yesterday but I think I messed up with the edits and be reported as spam","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26404-submariner93","name":"Submariner93","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26404_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-29T04:16:05-07:00","id":1533356,"title":"","content":"In VDA20 Humanity and True Faith are not linked. You can follow also other Paths. You can even be on the VDA20 Road of Set and believe that Set's job is to keep at bay evil forces, though through a weird and unconventional method, and thus have True Faith. The only important thing, as far as VDA20 Tome of Secrets is concerned, is that what you believe in has some role in fighting evil.<br /> <br /> At the same time VDA20 leaves True Faith entirely in ST hands: there is no merit for it and there are no mechanical standards, although there are mechanical effects (nice and well defined ones). Personally \u0130 don't like that and would have preferred a system behind it to reduce tensions between STs and players on whether a given character can have True Faith, and to avoid it being too powerful, too: 3-4 dots of TF give immunity to most disciplines. That's in addition to any xp a character might have. \u0130t's needlessly overpowered and \u0130 don't believe it's justified by &quot;it's so that it's acquisition is more about narrative and roleplaying than points&quot;, since that should be true for any dot and point on the character sheet, not just True Faith, and we already have huge mechanic aspects of the game like Road and Virtues or ideological disciplines like Bardo or disciplines that are accessed because of the agreement of some other supernatural party, like Dur-An-Ki (based on ancient deals with spirits) being well regulated mechanically and yet tied to narrative and roleplay. But that's my take, since \u0130 think the rule system is already designed to be narrative centred as it is, and does not require bypassing or blindspots (like leaving True Faith without good rules) to make the game work better.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-29T13:03:31-07:00","id":1533365,"title":"","content":"In my chronicles, low levels of True Faith (generally 1-5) can be held by just about anyone as long as their belief in the Godhead is sincere. Almost any true believer can hold a crucifix and repel a vampire, or say some sort of prayer and get some protection against an evil spirit. So it's no obstacle to have low levels of True Faith and still be a crusading knight who burns down the town and let God sort it out, or be an Islamist terrorist, or just be an ordinary person trying to get by.<br /> <br /> However, high levels of true faith (generally 6+) is not the province of the masses, but actual saints and mystics who are experiencing some sort of union with the Godhead. At that level of union, compassion and a sense of all humanity belonging together as one takes over. The individual ego erodes, temporal concerns wither, and self-sacrifice takes over. At these levels it is absolutely necessary for high Humanity and high True Faith to merge. But under True Faith 5? Not so much need to tie in high Humanity.<br /> <br /> It really comes down to whether we are talking about basic stuff seen in horror movies (holding up a cross to repel vampires) or actual miracles.<br /> <br /> I have no problems with lots of NPCs having low levels of True Faith, especially in a Dark Ages setting. In my Dark Ages chronicles, pretty much every NPC human will be able to hold up the cross and repel vampires because the Middle Ages are supposed to be the Age of Faith, and we should see the difference between that society and our more secular society. So True Faith 1 and 2 is had by almost everyone. Actual witch hunting inquisitors could very well have True Faith 3 and 4. But we won't see higher True Faith except from those are seen as actual living saints - and saints are typically only honored after they're gone. Saints are seen as troublemakers because they aren't willing to compromise on the radical teachings of God no matter how uncomfortable that makes people. They challenge people to make changes in their own behavior, and few people find that comfortable.<br /> <br /> Of course, I also restrict True Faith to the universal monotheist traditions. I don't allow things like True Faith in Set or the Flying Spaghetti Monster. I think that's just nonsense. For actual pagan religions or occult teachings, some sort of Sorcery or perhaps Numina is usually the better path. If you're a worshipper of Isis, the mysteries of Isis don't match the mechanics of True Faith, they match some kind of magical teaching. I try to match mechanics to the actual stories/legends of how that religion interacts with any kind of magic or miracles. If True Faith, or some modified version of it, best fits it, I use that. If Sorcery, Numina, use of Willpower as Forbiddance or Warding, a certain kind of Merit, etc. fits better, I use that. I'm sure there's some religion out there that doesn't map well to anything and would require more extensive thinking on my end, but it's never come up in game.<br /> <br /> Likewise, not everything involving monotheist religions involve True Faith. A priest performing the rite of exorcism likely has some measure of True Faith, but the actual rite itself is likely some form of Theurgy that is a Dismissal or Protection ritual of the Via Geniorum. Match the most appropriate mechanics to whatever the event is. If I wanted a high powered Dark Ages inquisitor who still does awful things, I'd keep the True Faith low and boost his Theurgy and Merits which are not co-dependent on Humanity. The old 1995 sourcebook Inquisition had a ton of cool merits and other powers that would be appropriate in these situations.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":"04-02-2024, 01:58 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}